\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Chapter 19 of Israel was a lament. It said as much, at the beginning, at the end. It was a lament in form, and it was a lament in reality. But it also subverted that lament form thoroughly.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Chapter 20 says it is an announcement of judgement. It is a history. It is a response to people come to inquire of the LORD. And yet, it is a response that refuses to give an answer, but instead obeys a command to announce judgement. It strays from announcing judgement to go into the history of the people - but it subverts that history when it does so.\
And so, we are well warned that things may not be as they seem as we read this chapter. We must apply extra caution, and make sure what we say about the text fits with the entire oracle. Perhaps the people had false ideas about inquiring of the LORD, about history, about God's judgement. But then, perhaps not everything we believe in those areas is true as well. We must be prepared to be corrected by the text.\
Our text starts with a date, and an event, grounding us in history. Yet we are told little of the event - elders sit down to inquire of the LORD. Perhaps we could discover what they meant by this, what they hoped to achieve, or what they were asking. I myself do not know. Ezekiel chooses not to foreground their question, but rather, the LORD's word, and the raw fact that it was the LORD's word. The LORD speaks, asking rhetorically if they are seeking or inquiring, and swears an oath: he will not be sought, inquired of. Not by them.\
If we do not understand what is meant by this, we will not understand the oracle. But we will need to resort to the entire history presented instead of the answer they were looking for, the entire judgement, the entire purpose of God revealed here.
