\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Looking at the opening, what strikes me most is the violence of God's refusal. The elders come 
\f2 \uc0\u1500 \u1460 \u1491 \u1456 \u1512 \u1465 \u1513 \u1473 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1462 \u1514 \u1470 \u1497 \u1456 \u1492 \u1493 \u1464 \u1492 
\f1  (to inquire of/seek the LORD), which elsewhere in scripture is presented as the right thing to do. David inquires of the LORD before battles. Kings seek prophetic guidance. Yet here God swears by His own life: 
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1460 \u1501 \u1470 \u1488 \u1460 \u1491 \u1464 \u1468 \u1512 \u1461 \u1513 \u1473 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1500 \u1464 \u1499 \u1462 \u1501 
\f1  - "if I will be inquired of by you" - meaning absolutely not.\
The repetition of the judgment question is forceful: 
\f2 \uc0\u1492 \u1458 \u1514 \u1460 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1508 \u1465 \u1468 \u1496 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1465 \u1514 \u1464 \u1501 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1492 \u1458 \u1514 \u1460 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1508 \u1468 \u1493 \u1465 \u1496 
\f1  - "Will you judge them? Will you judge?" This isn't asking Ezekiel if he's willing but commanding him to take the judge's seat. And specifically to judge them through 
\f2 \uc0\u1492 \u1493 \u1465 \u1491 \u1460 \u1497 \u1506 \u1461 \u1501 
\f1  - "making them know" - the abominations of their fathers.\
What puzzles me is verse 8's claim about Egypt: 
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1463 \u1497 \u1463 \u1468 \u1502 \u1456 \u1512 \u1493 \u1468 \u1470 \u1489 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1  - "they rebelled against me" by not throwing away the 
\f2 \uc0\u1513 \u1460 \u1473 \u1511 \u1468 \u1493 \u1468 \u1510 \u1461 \u1497 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1506 \u1461 \u1497 \u1504 \u1461 \u1497 \u1492 \u1462 \u1501 
\f1  (the detestable things of their eyes) and the 
\f2 \uc0\u1490 \u1460 \u1468 \u1500 \u1468 \u1493 \u1468 \u1500 \u1461 \u1497 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1502 \u1460 \u1510 \u1456 \u1512 \u1463 \u1497 \u1460 \u1501 
\f1  (the idols of Egypt). This isn't in Exodus. The standard narrative shows Israel groaning under bondage, crying out, being delivered. But here God says He nearly poured out His wrath on them IN Egypt for idolatry.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <notes> Key observations: - The refusal to be inquired of must connect to what follows - the history lesson is the answer to why they can't inquire - The history emphasizes rebellion at every stage, even before deliverance - God repeatedly acts "for my name's sake" 
\f2 \uc0\u1500 \u1456 \u1502 \u1463 \u1506 \u1463 \u1503 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1502 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1  rather than for Israel's merit - The pattern repeats with each generation: rebellion, near-destruction, preservation for the name's sake </notes>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The implication seems to be that their entire relationship with God has been built on His unilateral commitment to His name's reputation among the nations, not on any genuine seeking or obedience from them. They want to "inquire" as if they have standing to consult God, but the history shows they've never had such standing - only God's self-interested preservation has kept them alive.\
But I'm uncertain about why this history specifically prevents them from inquiring. Is it that they're trying to use God as an oracle while maintaining idolatry (verse 31 confirms they're still passing children through fire)? Or is the very act of inquiry presumptuous given their history?\
The text seems to be saying: you cannot approach me for guidance while your fundamental orientation remains rebellious. The inquiry itself becomes another form of idolatry - trying to use God's wisdom while rejecting His sovereignty.
