\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Judge's seat may not be entirely the right analogy, but something in that general direction is clearly happening. We will need to be careful with the word judge in this chapter - its meanings may not always be the same, as it can be a somewhat broad word. Here, it seems to commanding Ezekiel to indict the nation. It is a key word for the chapter, so we will need to track its use in individual sentences, in sections, and in the rhetoric as a whole.\
Verse 8's description is clearly and intentionally distinct from the description Exodus gives, as you say. The Torah accounts do not contradict verse 8, and they even hint at it, but that is not their focus or their emphasis. Ezekiel has left out much that Exodus focuses on. However, there is something in common between the book of Exodus and Ezekiel here. The vehemence and some but not all of the wording of the prohibition on idlos of Exodus 20 reappear in verse 7 of Ezekiel 20. Moreover, the vehemence and the prohibition seem to have been necessary - even as the commands are being given, in Exodus 32, the people have built themselves an idol.\
The phrase 'the idols of Egypt' is part of a larger rhetorical move where Ezekiel accuses the nation of having sin back to its early days. This is something he has already done - chapter 16 said that Jerusalem's parents were Amorite and Hittite, Canaanite peoples, pagans. And indeed, the rest of chapter 16 also emphasizes events in the nation's history which the people might have wished to forget. Even more, chapter 16 emphasized God's opinion on that pagan nature of the nation, and on the past events that resulted.\
And yet, there is a deeper unity between this part of Ezekiel and the book of Exodus. The LORD's self revelation, his making covenant with his people, his love for his people - these things stay the same, though they are expressed differently. Part of the reason is the larger situation Ezekiel is speaking into, part of the reason is doubtless that Moses and Ezekiel are different people - but the immediate reason is this inquiry by the elders. \
The elders, and the larger audience among the exiles, clearly have some rebellious misunderstanding of the situation, misunderstanding which the LORD corrects in this oracle. While previous chapters can give us the rough outline of that misunderstanding, exactly what the LORD is correcting here we must infer from the word by word communication choices in the text. \
But you are correct about the broad outlines. The people hold on to their idolatry and rebellion, and think that they survive this extremely dangerous practice and heart orientation for any reason other than God's care for his own name. The people think their history, their origins, their nation, gives them protection, rather than stored up iniquity crying out for punishment in justice.\
But, to understand this chapter, we will need to be a lot more detailed than that broad outline. What, from this first section through to verse 9, offers useful evidence to understand the LORD's purpose in doing the things narrated and in speaking in this way about them, in more exactness than we have so far?
