\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The eyes, as well as the heart, going whoring after idols was an allegation going back to Ezekiel 6. And as recently as chapter 18, lifting eyes to the idols was part of the definition by example of wickedness. The context there suggests orientation, but not precisely that of vision as such but of focus. Still, as you say, the comparison to the promised land is partially visual. But I think it encompasses other dimensions of subjective and objective desire as well. The land of Israel offered to them is desirable.\
Even more so than offering them a subjectively and objectively more desirable land, the LORD offered them himself, that is, knowledge of himself and relationship with himself. Having offered land, knowledge, and relationship, their turning their eyes to the objectively disgusting and less desirable idols is offensive. Moreover, it is rebellious - swearing to them 'I am your God' is not just an offer to take care of them. It implies a duty to loyal relationship on their part as well. That means no idols, and no approaching the LORD while defiled by idols. Thus, the commands to get rid of the idols, and stop defiling yourselves. But they would not. \
And yes, I do think the sum of the evidence of the entire chapter does point to the inquiry by the elders being mishandling of the relationship with God from their end. But before we get to the other sections of this chapter, I think we still need to look at how these elements of this section up through verse 9 contribute on a more detailed level to the message of this chapter.
