\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Looking more precisely at the mechanics of verses 5-9:\
The phrase 
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1464 \u1488 \u1462 \u1513 \u1464 \u1468 \u1474 \u1488 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1497 \u1464 \u1491 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1  - "I lifted my hand" - appears three times in quick succession (verses 5, 6). This is oath language, but the repetition is excessive. First He lifts His hand 
\f2 \uc0\u1500 \u1456 \u1494 \u1462 \u1512 \u1463 \u1506 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1489 \u1461 \u1468 \u1497 \u1514 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1497 \u1463 \u1506 \u1458 \u1511 \u1465 \u1489 
\f1  - "to the seed of the house of Jacob." Then He lifts it again 
\f2 \uc0\u1500 \u1461 \u1488 \u1502 \u1465 \u1512 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1458 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1497 \u1456 \u1492 \u1493 \u1464 \u1492 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1457 \u1500 \u1465 \u1492 \u1461 \u1497 \u1499 \u1462 \u1501 
\f1  - "saying 'I am YHWH your God.'" Then again to bring them out to the land. The redundancy suggests God is emphasizing the formal, legally binding nature of these commitments.\
The verb 
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1464 \u1488 \u1460 \u1493 \u1464 \u1468 \u1491 \u1463 \u1506 
\f1  - "I made myself known" - is reflexive/causative. God causes Himself to be known. This isn't Israel discovering God; it's God imposing knowledge of Himself on them. Yet immediately they must choose what to do with that imposed knowledge.\
The command structure in verse 7 is telling: 
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1460 \u1497 \u1513 \u1473 
\f1  - "each man" - makes this individual, not collective. Each person must cast away 
\f2 \uc0\u1513 \u1460 \u1473 \u1511 \u1468 \u1493 \u1468 \u1510 \u1461 \u1497 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1506 \u1461 \u1497 \u1504 \u1464 \u1497 \u1493 
\f1  - "the detestable things of his eyes." The possessive suffix makes these 
\i their
\i0  abominations, things they've already attached to. The command isn't "don't pick up Egyptian idols" but "throw away the ones you already treasure."\
Then comes 
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1468 \u1489 \u1456 \u1490 \u1460 \u1500 \u1468 \u1493 \u1468 \u1500 \u1461 \u1497 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1502 \u1460 \u1510 \u1456 \u1512 \u1463 \u1497 \u1460 \u1501 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1463 \u1500 \u1470 \u1514 \u1460 \u1468 \u1496 \u1463 \u1468 \u1502 \u1464 \u1468 \u1488 \u1493 \u1468 
\f1  - "don't defile yourselves with Egypt's idols." The shift from individual possessive (their detestable things) to national attribution (Egypt's idols) suggests two levels of idolatry - personal attachments and cultural participation.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <notes> The defilement language is crucial - 
\f2 \uc0\u1496 \u1502 \u1488 
\f1  implies ritual impurity that makes one unfit for sacred contact. This connects directly to why they can't inquire of God now. </notes>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Verse 8's rebellion is described as 
\f2 \uc0\u1500 \u1465 \u1488 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1464 \u1489 \u1493 \u1468 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1500 \u1460 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1502 \u1465 \u1506 \u1463 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1461 \u1500 \u1463 \u1497 
\f1  - "they were not willing to listen to me." Not that they couldn't, but wouldn't. This willful refusal while still in Egypt means they never accepted the basic terms of relationship even before deliverance.\
The phrase 
\f2 \uc0\u1500 \u1456 \u1502 \u1463 \u1506 \u1463 \u1503 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1502 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1  in verse 9 gains new weight in this context. God must act "for the sake of my name" precisely because Israel gives Him no other reason. The elders coming to inquire are operating from this same presumption - that God must respond because of His name's sake, not because they've met any relational obligations.\
This reframes the entire chapter: God is showing them that their current attempt to inquire while maintaining idolatry (verse 31 confirms this) is identical to their ancestors' behavior in Egypt - trying to have both YHWH and idols, presuming on God's self-interest in maintaining His reputation rather than actually entering proper relationship.\
The inquiry fails because it's built on the same foundational rebellion that's characterized the relationship from the start: wanting God's benefits while maintaining their "detestable things of their eyes."
