\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Reading the repetition of the oath language as excessive may be a sign we are missing something that repetition is intended to communicate. For instance, the genetic and cultural descent from Jacob is a substantial part of the incorrect beliefs this chapter is countering. And the book of Genesis repeatedly shows the LORD swearing oaths and confirming oaths about this matter to Abraham, then Isaac, then Jacob. How does this section effectively recast those oaths to the patriarchs on which the people might have based false hopes? \
Similarly, the other two oaths are directly tied to that imposing knowledge of the LORD with its accompanying response which you mentioned, and the identity and role of the LORD as God with its accompanying response. That knowledge of the LORD is clearly a major theme of the chapter. And the role of the LORD as the God of Israel is both a major theme of the chapter and a basis for false hopes. How does this section effectively recast those oaths?\
Why do you think the mention of Egypt as such refers to national level or cultural level problems, rather than pagan origin? Is there, anywhere else in the chapter, such a concern? If there is, that should be included more thoroughly in our understanding of the rhetoric, though with whatever nuance the other uses and the role they play in the rest of the chapter would give those national or cultural concerns.\
What are some possibilities for the role the individual nature of the command in verse 7 might be playing in the rhetoric there?
