\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Torah itself had discussed the nature of the promise made to Abraham - and that, with the word 'chosen', which seems rather key to this section as well. Genesis 18:19 has the LORD say of Abraham "For I have chosen him, that he may command his children and his household after him to keep the way of the Lord by doing righteousness and justice, so that the Lord may bring to Abraham what he has promised him."\
That word 'chosen' gets at the issue of election you raised as well.\
That exodus identity you mentioned was a massive factor in the historical reputation of the LORD among the nations. In demanding the Exodus from Egypt, and then forcing it, the LORD attached his reputation to the fate of Israel. For centuries, the LORD was known as the one who could bring that kind of force to bear.\
That reputation, that name, is a key theme in the other sections of history reinterpreted by Ezekiel as well. But we have a new element - Sabbath. What are some possibilities for the role played by Sabbath in this section, and what is the evidence for and against each?
