\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 As evidence for the visible covenant marker, the Sabbath is explicitly called a sign. You are correct to note that the nations seeing is not explicitly mentioned. But visibility as covenant marker to Israel may need to be part of our final theory.\
The purpose clause is strong evidence for the identity possibility. Moreover, it goes some way towards answering your question, 'why Sabbath?' Sabbath is not just a marker of the covenant in some vague generic way here. The gift of Sabbath is part of the LORD's self revelation, a sign that the LORD has chosen to sanctify Israel. \
Now, it is true that many of the commands demanded that Israel be holy. Much of the Leviticus legislation says Israel must be holy because the LORD is holy. But the Sabbath is a gift of sacred time itself in every week, every span of seven years, every Jubilee. \
Whether they sanctified that time themselves or violated the Sabbath commands, the Sabbath was a sign to them that the LORD had chosen this people. Other nations might need to work on a short time scale, and make investments on a long time scale. But to get the same benefits and more, the LORD's chosen people need merely do nothing, trusting in the LORD who chose them, and gave them this gift. Being in faithful covenant relationship with the LORD, nothing else required, would see them given prosperity in those particular days and years. Thus, they might come to know the LORD as the source of all prosperity on this planet, and specifically as the one who had chosen to set apart their particular nation for himself, blessing it in the process. \
But the nation would not thus trust, not thus obey. But the other sections of this history do not frame this disobedience as a lack of trust. They frame it as desecration of what is holy, and a failure to sanctify, that is, treat as holy, that which is holy. So while there are elements of trust and anti-idolatry to the Sabbath, I think you are correct in what you find most compelling. The Sabbath is a marker of the LORD as the one who sanctifies. The purpose statement makes that clear, and when the Sabbath is brought up again and again later, it reinforces that clarity. They were to be holy, chosen by God, set apart from the nations for God's purposes. But it is not the Sabbath that would give them standing to approach the LORD. The Sabbath is the sign pointing to the much larger reality, the LORD who not only is holy himself but makes holy whom he chooses.
