\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Because the Sinai generation thus rejected that sanctification, that relationship with the LORD, the LORD would have ended them on that basis. But, for the sake of his reputation among the nations, the LORD did not do so. But, that they deserved that destruction is key to Ezekiel's argument.\
The next generation too, made the wrong choice and deserved punishment. As with previous chapters in Ezekiel, their fathers sins did not prevent them from repenting and choosing life - but nonetheless there is a real tendency for children to follow the ways of their fathers, and so it was here. Like their fathers, the next generation disobeyed the LORD's command, desecrated the LORD's Sabbaths, and turned their eyes to idols - the very idols of their fathers. With the mention of scattering among the nations, we lean that this generation deserves the fate that befell the exiles that are Ezekiel's audience. But, like with the fathers of the second generation, the LORD withholds his hand and spares that generation too for the sake of his name.\
But the people do not escape entirely. The LORD gives them no-good decrees, and statues which cannot give life. The LORD defiles them through child sacrifice. Look at the purpose clause for this - that the LORD might devastate them. As before, the ultimate purpose is that they might know the LORD is the LORD. But the means are a hard word to hear.\
Both because these means are uncomfortable, and because they are objectively difficult to interpret, we must use additional caution. Moreover, we must consider the contrast with earlier, similar language. Verse 13 has already accused the people of rejecting the decrees given at Sinai, describing them as decrees by which a person doing them will live. They are further distinguished by being given masculine grammatical gender. They are moreover, contrasted by being given to the second generation.\
Perhaps Ezekiel had specific God given decrees in mind, such as the way Moses consulted the LORD when handling disputes among the people during that time period. But that would not explain the child sacrifice - Moses would hardly have permitted such.\
Perhaps Ezekiel had in mind misunderstandings of the law or outright violation. That could explain the child sacrifice, but it dodges the force of the direct statements that the LORD gave these decrees, and that he did so to devastate.\
Perhaps Ezekiel had in mind the decrees by which the LORD rescued his people during that time, rather than ending them like they deserved. That would, in the end, lead to a greater devastation as the people responded to that patience by adding to their iniquity. But this possibility contradicts the description of these rules as not being life giving. \
But perhaps Ezekiel simply does not have any specific thing in mind, but refers more generally to all of the LORD's ways with his people during that time period. This in no way softens the blow - if the sum of the LORD's ways towards his people did not lead to life, the LORD defiled his people by child sacrifice even if he also forbade such, and the purpose of all of this was to devastate his people, this is still quite shocking. \
All of these interpretations have defects such that not only should we consider the evidence for and against them, and their potential place in Ezekiel's rhetoric, we should look for either alternatives that fit the evidence better, or even simply hints that Ezekiel has left, oddities in the text which might lead to better understanding.
