\f1  - "and also I" - in verse 25 parallels verse 23's "also I lifted my hand to scatter them." Both are divine responses to the second generation's rebellion, suggesting these "not good statutes" are part of the same judgment as the scattering threat.\
The crucial oddity is verse 26's language: 
\f2 \uc0\u1489 \u1456 \u1468 \u1492 \u1463 \u1506 \u1458 \u1489 \u1460 \u1497 \u1512 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1499 \u1464 \u1468 \u1500 \u1470 \u1508 \u1462 \u1468 \u1496 \u1462 \u1512 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1512 \u1464 \u1495 \u1463 \u1501 
\f1  - "in passing through every opener of the womb." This directly echoes Exodus 13:12's legitimate command about dedicating firstborns to God: 
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1492 \u1463 \u1506 \u1458 \u1489 \u1463 \u1512 \u1456 \u1514 \u1464 \u1468 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1499 \u1464 \u1500 \u1470 \u1508 \u1462 \u1468 \u1496 \u1462 \u1512 \u1470 \u1512 \u1462 \u1495 \u1462 \u1501 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1500 \u1463 \u1497 \u1492 \u1493 \u1464 \u1492 
\f1  - "you shall pass over every opener of the womb to YHWH."\
This suggests something more subtle and terrible than God giving evil commands. God's legitimate command about firstborn dedication - meant to commemorate the Exodus and acknowledge God's claim on Israel - becomes the vehicle for their defilement when practiced by people whose hearts are after idols.\
The key might be 
\f2 \uc0\u1489 \u1456 \u1468 \u1502 \u1463 \u1514 \u1456 \u1468 \u1504 \u1493 \u1465 \u1514 \u1464 \u1501 
\f1  - "through their gifts." The possessive suggests these are offerings they chose to make, but God "defiled them" through these very offerings. How? Perhaps when hearts oriented toward idols perform even legitimate rituals, those rituals become corrupted. They transform God's command about firstborn redemption into child sacrifice - taking what should affirm life (redeeming the firstborn) and turning it into death.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <notes> This interpretation would explain: - Why the statutes are "not good" - not inherently evil but become destructive in rebellious hands - The feminine gender - perhaps referring to the corrupted form rather than the original commands - The connection to child sacrifice while maintaining God gave these statutes - The devastating irony that their religious observance becomes their defilement </notes>\
