\cf2 Evidence supporting this reading: Verse 24 emphasizes 
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1488 \u1463 \u1495 \u1458 \u1512 \u1461 \u1497 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1490 \u1460 \u1468 \u1500 \u1468 \u1493 \u1468 \u1500 \u1461 \u1497 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1458 \u1489 \u1493 \u1465 \u1514 \u1464 \u1501 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1492 \u1464 \u1497 \u1493 \u1468 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1506 \u1461 \u1497 \u1504 \u1461 \u1497 \u1492 \u1462 \u1501 
\f1  - "their eyes were after their fathers' idols." With eyes oriented toward idols, they inevitably corrupt whatever commands they receive.\
The purpose clause 
\f2 \uc0\u1500 \u1456 \u1502 \u1463 \u1506 \u1463 \u1503 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1458 \u1513 \u1460 \u1473 \u1502 \u1461 \u1468 \u1501 
\f1  - "in order to devastate/appall them" - suggests God allowed this corruption to reach its full expression so they would recognize the horror of what they'd become. Only by seeing their "gifts" produce defilement rather than sanctification might they recognize 
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1458 \u1513 \u1462 \u1473 \u1512 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1458 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1497 \u1456 \u1492 \u1493 \u1464 \u1492 
\f1  - "that I am YHWH" - the true God they'd abandoned.\
This reading makes verse 25-26 not about God giving inherently evil commands, but about God allowing His good commands to become instruments of judgment when perverted by idolatrous hearts. The elders sitting before Ezekiel likely still practice such corrupted worship (verse 31 confirms ongoing child sacrifice), thinking they're honoring God while actually defiling themselves through twisted versions of divine commands.\
