The crimes of the people living in the land, that is, between the wilderness wanderings and the exile, are summed up in one word - treason, though one could equally say they are summed up by the word blasphemy. Verse 27 does both.\
The nature of this treachery is fairly straightforward - sacrifices to other gods. The LORD sought out a beautiful land for his people, flowing with good things to eat. But the very things that make the land beautiful, hills and trees, are used as places to commit treason. And the good things to eat are being offered to other gods. \
The language of high hills and trees recalls Deuteronomy 12:2, in which the people were ordered to destroy the pagan shrines to pagan gods on the high mountains, on the hills, and under every green tree. Ezekiel 6:13 may also be referenced - it uses the words high hill, tree, leafy, and pleasing aroma from Ezekiel 20, while also using the mountaintops of Deuteronomy 12:2 - "And you shall know that I am the Lord, when their slain lie among their idols around their altars, on every high hill, on all the mountaintops, under every green tree, and under every leafy oak, wherever they offered pleasing aroma to all their idols."\
Whatever benefit, right, or hope the people might have gotten out of their history of worship to the LORD is undercut by a simple question - what is this high place to which you go? This is driven into memory by a pun, an assonance. What is the high place, bamah, to which they go? Bamah, Ba Ma, 'to go' 'what'. That this is its name to this very day says that the crimes of the recent past are ongoing. What is this high place then, on account of the crimes committed there? Treachery, blasphemy, provocation, things the audience still does.\
This point was made memorable in verse 29, but it is made explicit in verse 30. 'Are you defiling yourselves the way your ancestors did?'\
As in the other discussions of history and generational sin in Ezekiel, the present generation is continuing to sin. Their ancestors sin removes any hope from past generations. Their present sin removes any hope coming from the present generation.\
