\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 In one sense they were brazen and unreasonable, and Ezekiel calls them out on this. The evidence was there. Moreover, in the face of prophets warning them, Ezekiel included, they were defiant. But yes, in another sense, their thought, their practice, their worldview from which these things come, was normal - that is, normal to the environment of the nations they were among. \
To understand this, think about the environment we both live in. We are surrounded by the genuine advances of the Enlightenment - but also by pride in the Enlightenment and propaganda for it. We are surrounded by the genuine advances of science - but also by pride in it and propaganda for a twisted version of science. We are surrounded by the genuine advances of democracy - but also by pride in it and propaganda for a twisted version of democracy. All this is normal, and it is easy to treat it as the truth - but we must follow what is true, not what is easy.\
In the same way, Ezekiel's audience was surrounded by nations with literature, commerce, armies, imagery, and religious practices flooding in from all sides. There were real tangible benefits to becoming like the nations, and more that were not real if you believed the standard worldview. Both by their desire to become like the nations, and by the rhetoric the book of Ezekiel uses to try to persuade them, we see that to a great degree they did believe the prevailing worldview.\
But they were incorrect; Ezekiel had the truth, and they did not. That is to say, the LORD was not who they thought he was, in that the LORD was far more than they thought he was, in his strength, his passion, and consequently, his fury.\
As a result, they were incorrect in another common way. They misinterpreted the LORD's choice not to destroy their people in the past, failing badly because they oversimplified the hypothesis space. Past lack of disaster is only evidence of future lack of disaster to the degree that one would not expect a state which will cause disaster in the future to not have caused disaster in the past. Formally, that is just Bayes' theorem applied to this case; the evidence ratio to update towards being in a particular future disaster causing state has as its numerator the probability of having seen no disaster in the past if one were in that state. In the case where one would have definitely seen no past disaster despite a future disaster causing state, that simplifies to no evidence whatsoever against future disaster.\
In the case of Ezekiel 20, the audience thought the LORD was the sort of pagan god who would not destroy his people. That he had not destroyed his people time and time again might seem to be evidence of this. But Ezekiel counters this; he explains the reasons why the LORD had not destroyed his people, why those reasons no longer protected them, and why those same reasons now would lead to the LORD's fury against his people. See verses 33 through 38.\
I call this error a common error precisely because people continue to make a similar error today. Whether the disaster is the LORD's judgement on the whole Earth, or AI caused disaster, or some smaller scale disaster, people regularly resort to implicit or explicit trust in the disaster not having happened yet. They do not say 'have circumstances changed such that what once did not cause disaster now will?' They take comfort in the long period in which disaster of whatever kind has not happened. This is common, but careful reasoners ought not to fall into the trap because others do. Instead, we ought to reason precisely from the evidence, including that of Ezekiel, how his words were fulfilled, and how he told us that fulfillment was to be interpreted when it was made manifest.
