\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 This logic leads to a small gleam of hope. Ezekiel has been contending that the idolatry of the people is unacceptable to the LORD, and will lead to fury. In the way he words the desire of his audience to be like the nations in having idols, serving wood and stone, he echoes the warning in Deuteronomy 4 against idolatry, but also the prediction there that this idolatry would lead to death, destruction, and exile. But Deuteronomy also holds out hope for restoration. It is precisely the condition for this restoration which is key to understanding this chapter: seeking the LORD with all their heart and all their soul. This entire oracle was occasioned by the elders seeking the LORD - but very much not with all their heart and all their soul. They were still serving wood and stone, as Deuteronomy 4 predicted they would even in exile.\
And so, purging, as in verse 38, is not just a response in fury to rebellion. It creates the conditions for seeking the LORD, and thus for the renewal of verses 39 through 44.
