\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 While Egypt was a place to which many of the Jewish people were exiled, this is not what the wilderness of the land of Egypt refers to; it is explicitly referred to as the experience of their fathers. Exodus 13 tells of the LORD personally leading the people through the wilderness toward the Red Sea. This was before the great deliverance at the Red Sea, the LORD's mighty arm stretched out against Egypt. The king of Egypt thought the people were wandering and trapped by the Sea. But they exactly where the LORD wanted them, and they walked through the sea on dry land. To some degree, I gave a misleading impression when I spoke of Egypt as not physically wilderness, as the king of Egypt would have considered that physical territory his own.\
